<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Reichstag Fire Decree</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1933">1933</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> Fire Decree was an emergency decree issued by President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-von-hindenburg">Paul von Hindenburg</a> on 28 February 1933, the day after the Reichstag building was set on fire. It suspended civil liberties such as freedom of speech, the press and assembly and allowed detention without trial, and it was used at once to arrest communists and other opponents. It remained in force for the whole Nazi period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/reichstag-fire-decree.jpg" alt="Reichstag Fire Decree" width="320" height="483" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Verboten_Zeitung_1933.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>By the early 1930s successive presidential cabinets of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a> governed through emergency decrees under Article 48 of the constitution rather than through stable parliamentary majorities. Article 48 allowed the president, where public order and security were seriously disturbed or endangered, to take emergency measures and suspend specific rights, and it let the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> revoke them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> became Chancellor on 30 January 1933 at the head of a coalition with the German National People's Party, and new elections were called for 5 March.</p><p>On the evening of 27 February 1933 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> building in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> was set on fire. The circumstances of the fire remain controversial among historians, but there is broad agreement that the Nazi leadership exploited it at once. Hitler and his supporters blamed the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and presented the fire as the signal for a Communist uprising. Dozens of Communists were arrested within hours.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Hindenburg signed the decree on 28 February 1933, and Hitler, Interior Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-frick">Wilhelm Frick</a> and Justice Minister Franz Gürtner countersigned it. Its official title was the Decree of the Reich President for the Protection of People and State. Section 1 suspended articles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-constitution">Weimar Constitution</a> protecting personal liberty, the home, the privacy of mail and telecommunications, freedom of expression and of the press, freedom of assembly and association, and property rights, 'until further notice'. Sections 2 and 3 empowered the Reich government to intervene in state governments that failed to restore public safety and order. Sections 4 and 5 set severe penalties for resisting the decree and extended the death penalty to several offences, including arson and sabotage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The decree was applied at once. Thousands of Communists and Social Democrats were arrested, and opposition meetings and newspapers were banned. The heaviest early repression came in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermann-goring">Hermann Göring</a> controlled the largest police force in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and on 3 March he instructed the police to use the decree first against Communists. Ernst Thälmann was detained, while Wilhelm Pieck and Walter Ulbricht escaped arrest.</p><p>The decree prepared the way for the Enabling Act of 23 March 1933, which passed with many Communist and Social Democratic deputies arrested, expelled or in hiding. Evans argued that the decree left the states no longer properly constituted, making the Act's passage irregular. The decree remained in force throughout the Third Reich and was never formally revoked by the regime. Its use later led the framers of the postwar Basic Law to curtail sharply the powers of the federal president.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Reichstag Fire Decree” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Reichstag fire of 27 February the <b>Reichstag Fire Decree</b> suspended civil liberties, and on 23 March the Enabling Act gave the cabinet power to make laws without parliament; only the Social Democrats voted against it, the Communist deputies having already been arrested or driven into hiding.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Reichstag fire of 27 February 1933 the <b>Reichstag Fire Decree</b> suspended civil liberties.</blockquote><a href="/empire/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
